WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Hi there everyone, you might remember a while ago

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:09.148
we made a video about a new world record prime number, millions of digits long. Matt Parker

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.500
even printed it out for us and it didn't take just one volume,

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.899
it took three volumes. I've still got it here.

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:27.000
Lots and lots of digits. The typeface is tiny and it's page after page after page of digits.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.100
Now, here's a question

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:35.759
how many times does the number 7 appear in this prime number? Let's go, let's go to page 1, line 1.

00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:40.079
The 5th digit is a 7, and

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:47.909
there are many, many more on the first line and many, many, more on every line after that. In fact, there are well over

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:53.369
2 million 7s in this prime number. It's not surprising.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.849
There are a lot of digits. Now when numbers get this big primes become - well they become pretty sparse.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.199
So here's another question

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:04.199
is there a prime number,

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:09.509
bigger than this one, so taking more pages, more digits,

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:12.719
that has no sevens in it?

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:17.879
It's almost unthinkable isn't it, that you could have a number bigger than this, that's also prime,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.040
that has no sevens in it.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:26.069
But do you know what the answer is? It's not only yes the answer is there are an infinite number of prime numbers

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:31.679
bigger than this one that contain no sevens. This has been proven,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.610
it was proven by a mathematician called James Maynard at Oxford University

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.310
and we're about to speak to him now.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.320
(Brady: We're talking about your favourite subject)
- Primes!
- (Brady: Yes)

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:52.049
Okay. So today we're thinking about the digits of prime numbers and, in particular, are there infinitely many prime numbers with no

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:59.009
sevens anywhere in their digits? Or is it the case that there's only finitely many primes that have no sevens anywhere in their digits?

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.319
So this might sound like a pretty easy question, lots of prime numbers

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:10.739
that we're used to have no sevens in their digits. So 13 has no sevens in the digits,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.920
23 has no sevens in the digits, and

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.620
when we're looking at small numbers

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:19.949
lots and lots of small numbers have no sevens in their digits.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.350
But when you're talking about infinitely many numbers

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:25.708
you have to talk about really big numbers and

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:30.630
the vast, vast majority of really big numbers have lots and lots of sevens in their digits,

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:35.549
and so you're really looking at only a very small set of numbers that have no sevens.

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:41.069
So one way to measure how big a set is is to think about summing 1 over the numbers.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.679
So for example, you could choose the prime numbers,

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.860
it's a famous result that if you sum up 1 over 3, plus 1 over 7, plus 1 over

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:54.839
11, and so on; this diverges and so, becomes bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:55.890
So in one way

00:02:55.890 --> 00:03:00.779
this is saying that there's quite a lot of prime numbers, even though they're quite a sparse set in their whole numbers.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:02.970
But if I sum up

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:05.620
1 over n,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.609
for n having no sevens in its decimal expansion

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:11.429
then this total sum is less than

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:16.709
100. By this metric it's actually quite a small set of numbers and smaller than the set of the primes.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:24.660
So you might think of primes as quite rare and difficult to find, whereas numbers with no sevens anywhere in their decimal expansion has been

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.449
quite easy to find, we see them all the time.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:29.969
But actually when you get to really big numbers

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:35.759
the primes are much much more common than the numbers with no sevens in their decimal expansion.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:40.289
And it's always a big problem for people like me who like to study the primes as

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.699
to - kind of work out if there's infinitely many prime' in these

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:49.400
different odd sets which are small subsets of the numbers.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.800
(Brady: We all know there are an infinite number of prime numbers)
- Yep

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.170
(Brady: But you're saying do we get to a point where the prime numbers become so big,)

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.809
(and with so many digits it's impossible to ever find one without a seven again?)

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:10.010
Yeah, so is it the case that every large enough prime number always has at least one seven somewhere in its decimal expansion?

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.470
You'd certainly expect that most prime numbers that are really big would have lots of sevens in their decimal expansion,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.779
maybe it could be the case that you can never get ones that are only made up of the digits

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.809
one, two, three, four, five, six, eight, nine, zero;

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.539
one step bigger than a gazillion or something like that. Similarly, you can ask the same thing,

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:30.780
are there

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.040
infinitely many primes that have no zeros in their decimal expansion?

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.830
Are there infinitely many primes that have no ones in their decimal expansion?

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.399
All the problems are pretty similar to one another and similarly

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.729
regardless of whether it's no sevens or no zeros or no ones,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.649
we know that the sum of the reciprocals of those numbers is always bounded by 100 again.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:49.700
And so, again,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:54.259
it's always a fairly small set and so you expect all really large numbers, or

00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:00.469
most really large numbers, to have lots of 7s and lots of ones and lots of zeros in their decimal expansion.

00:05:00.469 --> 00:05:02.540
(Brady: And James, you cracked this nut?)

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:03.740
Right. Yeah

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:04.920
So I show you that

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:12.199
there are indeed infinitely many primes that have no sevens anywhere in their decimal expansion and again, instead of seven

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.710
you can substitute two,

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:18.200
or three or whatever number you like. Regardless how far you go along the number line,

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:25.159
so you can go some huge way along and you choose your favourite point, and then certainly somewhere beyond there

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.909
there's going to be some prime which has no

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:36.230
sevens and then another prime which has no threes, and so on. 
- (Brady: Because they're an infinite number of primes,)

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.309
(this feels intuitively correct doesn't it? You know, because there's always another number.)

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:46.280
(Did it feel intuitively correct to you before you proved it?)
- Even though, in this sense,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.609
the number of numbers with no sevens is quite small,

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:55.099
it's not so small that you would think it's that bizarre that you'd be able to find primes.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:01.249
The difficult is always showing it. The primes are really difficult to get your hands on and so the question is always,

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:07.509
can I understand the primes well enough to be able to find them in these somewhat strange

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:11.799
small sets of integers?
- (Brady: So if there are an infinite number of numbers)

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.880
(with no sevens. Proven, well done.)
- Yeah 
- (Brady: Well done)

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.120
(Are there an infinite number of primes,)

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:25.089
(with no sevens or threes? Can I, can I have two numbers that are missing from my prime?)

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.929
So this is an open problem. We don't know this at all,

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.700
however

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:37.029
we would certainly expect that there should be infinitely many times with no sevens or threes.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:43.390
You have to be a bit careful about how far you go along, because you can't have primes that have no

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:50.860
ones, twos threes, fours, fives, six, sevens or nines 'cause then they'd only be made out of zeros. Or, you can't have primes

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.300
that're just made out of twos for example, so there's infinitely many numbers that are just made out of twos,

00:06:55.610 --> 00:07:00.580
but all of them are even so you're certainly not going to get any primes in there 
- (Brady: Other than 2)
- Other than 2!

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.850
Good point you caught me. And it's a quite a famous problem

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:07.510
actually, are there infinitely many primes that are just the number one repeated?

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:11.559
So you certainly can't have it when you have a number of digits which is a multiple of three,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.630
because you know that if the sum of the digits adds up to a multiple of three then and the number itself is a multiple

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:21.070
of three, and so it can't be a prime. Nonetheless you think that - you would have guessed that

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:27.399
there should be a infinite number of primes that are just only made up of the digit one,

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:32.620
but this would be false for the digit two 
- (Brady: But the only candidates would be one...)

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:39.760
(seven maybe? Like, like it can't be an even because it can't be all twos, fours, sixes or eights.)

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:46.599
(Can't be zeroes, can't be threes, can't be nines because that would add up to - )
- Exactly, then it'd be a multiple of three

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.790
(Brady: Could it be all sevens?)

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:53.799
All sevens sounds good to me. Yeah. 
- (Brady: So ones and sevens are the only candidates for,)

00:07:54.590 --> 00:08:00.130
(mmm, what's that called mono digit?)
- I think they're called repunit? Maybe? 
- (Brady: That's it, yeah)

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.679
Yeah, so repunit primes. So I think ones and sevens are the only possibilities for that.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:07.580
(Brady: Oh, you totally have to get onto that one!)

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:08.080
Okay,

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.359
so that one feels a lot harder, that somehow

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:17.919
just missing one digit in base 10 is right at the limit of the current techniques and even just missing two digits, even though that

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:22.739
should clearly be possible, already feels quite a long way beyond all the techniques that we know at the moment;

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.210
because these sets get smaller and smaller and smaller too you look at bigger and bigger numbers.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.210
(Brady: When I heard that you'd proven this, this digit thing, you know about a missing digit,)

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:39.629
(I thought maybe there would be an exception like two or four but they all fall into it, whether they're even or odd or?)

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:45.119
Yeah, so there's a few subtleties. The last digit of a prime number is restricted

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:51.599
so, you know that the last digit of a prime number certainly can't be an even number. It can't be a five,

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.520
and so this changes slightly how many primes you'd expect there to be that have

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:03.480
no sevens or no twos. You'd expect there to be slightly different numbers because of this last digit phenomenon. And similarly

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:09.000
there are some other constraints, say to do with the sum of the digits, that interact with being a prime or not.

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:13.559
But regardless of these small complications it turns out that there's

00:09:13.839 --> 00:09:20.600
enough numbers are missing a digit that, regardless of what digit it is that you're missing, you can still find primes in them.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.520
(Brady: James I know you've been really involved in work on)

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:29.909
(the twin prime conjecture, and gaps between primes, and these are some of the most famous, glamorous problems in mathematics.)

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:38.300
(Is this a glamorous or important one or was this almost like just a little curio you found along the way? Or has this got significance?

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:45.119
So I find it a fun problem to state but as stated it feels a bit more like a recreational problem.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.459
But what really interests me is

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:53.460
the underlying techniques that go into the proof. The proof was actually quite complicated and it was

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:59.700
developing new tools to try and get a handle on primes. This was a fun application of these ideas

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:06.990
but maybe the long-term goal is that the tools that are developed to get a handle on primes that work in this setting can

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:14.490
maybe one day be developed enough to get a handle on primes in more complicated settings like the twin prime conjecture or something.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.770
We've been working in base 10 so far for our discussion, because that's the thing that's most familiar to us,

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:25.949
but you could ask the same question in any other base. The question gets easier as the bases get bigger.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:31.140
And so it also follows that you can find infinitely many primes that are missing

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.289
a given digit in any base bigger than equal to ten.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:40.259
And actually if you look at large enough bases, you can start avoiding more than one number.

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.859
So yeah, if you want to avoid twos and sevens,

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:48.389
we guess that should work in base ten, but we don't know how to prove it,

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.879
but we can prove it in a big enough base. So maybe base 100 would work. Maybe it needs to be a bit bigger

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:57.419
I'm not totally sure. You can ask the same question as well for smaller bases

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:04.770
but as I said, it gets harder for smaller bases and so base ten is, at the moment, right at the threshold of our techniques.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:09.300
It's quite fortunate that the threshold is the most natural one for us,

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:14.879
but one famous conjecture is that there's infinitely many Mersenne primes.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:18.089
So these are primes which of the form

00:11:19.029 --> 00:11:23.010
2 to the N, minus 1 and

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:29.669
these are interesting because often these are very easy to test for whether such numbers are prime or not,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:30.850
so lots of these

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:36.390
records for the biggest primes are all of the form 2 to the n minus 1. You can think about this as a problem

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:43.619
in base 2, and then in base 2 any Mersenne prime would just be the digit 1 repeated.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:48.060
So it would be a repdigit prime in base 2, or equivalently

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:55.289
it'd be a prime that's missing the digit 0 in base 2. So, these questions about primes missing digits

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.110
actually relate to some of these really long old

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:00.750
standing conjectures about

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.909
primes of special forms like Mersenne primes.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:11.209
(Brady: James I've seen you give a lecture about this before, and now you've done a video about it here with me, and each time)

00:12:11.209 --> 00:12:14.448
(you choose the number 7. You talk about how many primes don't have a 7.)

00:12:14.699 --> 00:12:17.299
(Why do you choose 7 as your arbitrary example?)

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.680
I always seem to choose 7 and I don't really know why I choose 7,

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:25.519
any other number would have been just as good. I've certainly read online that

00:12:26.069 --> 00:12:32.479
when people are asked to choose a one digit number, they disproportionately choose 7, and when they choose two digit numbers

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:34.699
they disproportionately choose 37 I think.

00:12:35.339 --> 00:12:43.189
So there's supposed to be some psychological thing about what seems more random and more like a non specifically chosen number,

00:12:43.529 --> 00:12:47.929
but I guess I'm always biased to choose a prime number. Yeah, I often like - given

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:52.430
silly questions when people ask me to choose a random number, I often choose one.

00:12:53.459 --> 00:12:56.959
Just to be difficult because this is the sort of thing that I find funny

00:12:57.959 --> 00:13:04.789
If you want to find out more about Professor Maynard, including his favourite number check out the latest edition of the Numberphile podcast,

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:09.259
I'll link to it on the screen and down below. And there'll also be links to more videos with him

00:13:09.259 --> 00:13:11.259
so check out those links as well.

